Pipeline in Vertex AI — In Depth

A pipeline is like an automatic production line for your Machine Learning project.

Instead of manually doing:

----------------

Prepare data → Train model → Test model → Deploy model

----------------

one by one, you define the complete workflow once.

Then the pipeline can execute the steps in the correct order.

=========

🏭 Real-Life Example: Bakery Production

Imagine a bakery.

🥣 Ingredients
      ↓
👨‍🍳 Prepare Dough
      ↓
🔥 Bake
      ↓
🎂 Check Quality
      ↓
📦 Pack
      ↓
🚚 Deliver

This is a bakery pipeline.

Each step has a specific job.

You cannot normally do:

Deliver
   ↓
Bake

The steps need to happen in the correct sequence.

The same idea applies to Machine Learning pipelines.

🤖 Machine Learning Pipeline

Suppose you want to create:

AI to predict tomorrow's bakery sales

Your pipeline might look like this:

📊 STEP 1
Collect Sales Data
        ↓
🧹 STEP 2
Clean the Data
        ↓
🔄 STEP 3
Prepare Features
        ↓
🧠 STEP 4
Train Model
        ↓
🧪 STEP 5
Evaluate Model
        ↓
🚀 STEP 6
Deploy Model

Let's understand each step.

📊 Step 1: Dataset

First, your AI needs data.

=========

| Date    | Product | Sales | Weather | Festival |
| ------- | ------- | ----: | ------- | -------- |
| Monday  | Cake    |    50 | Sunny   | No       |
| Tuesday | Cake    |    40 | Rainy   | No       |
| Sunday  | Cake    |   100 | Sunny   | Yes      |


This data might come from:

CSV files
BigQuery
Cloud Storage
Database
APIs
Pipeline flow
Raw Data
   ↓
Pipeline reads the data

=========

🧹 Step 2: Data Preparation

Real-world data is often messy.

For example:

Cake sales = 50
Cake sales = empty ❌
Cake sales = -100 ❌
Cake sales = fifty ❌

The pipeline can process the data.

Raw Data
     ↓
Remove incorrect data
     ↓
Handle missing values
     ↓
Convert text/numbers
     ↓
Clean Dataset

The result:

Clean data ready for training

=============

🔄 Step 3: Feature Engineering

A feature is information the AI uses to learn.

For bakery sales prediction:

Date
Day of Week
Weather
Festival
Previous Day Sales
Product Type

The pipeline can transform raw information into useful inputs.

Example:

Date: 2026-08-15
        ↓
Extract:
Day = Saturday
Month = August
Festival = Independence Day

The AI can now learn patterns such as:

"On weekends and festivals, cake sales usually increase."

=========

🧠 Step 4: Model Training

Now the cleaned data goes to the model.

Training Data
      ↓
Machine Learning Algorithm
      ↓
Model learns patterns
      ↓
Trained Model

For example:

Past Sales + Weather + Festival
                ↓
          ML Model Learns
                ↓
      Predict Future Sales

The model might learn:

Rainy Tuesday → Lower walk-in sales

Festival Sunday → Higher cake sales

========

🧪 Step 5: Model Evaluation

Now we test:

How good is the model?

Example:

Actual Sales:    100 cakes
Predicted Sales: 95 cakes

The pipeline calculates performance metrics such as:

MAE
RMSE
Accuracy
Precision
Recall

The metric depends on the type of ML problem.

For a sales prediction/regression model:

RMSE ↓ Lower is generally better
MAE  ↓ Lower is generally better

For a classification model:

Accuracy ↑ Higher is generally better
Precision ↑
Recall ↑

=========

🚀 Step 6: Model Deployment

If the model performs well:

Trained Model
      ↓
Evaluation Passed ✅
      ↓
Deploy to Endpoint
      ↓
Application can use the model

Now your website or application can send new data:

Today's Weather
      +
Tomorrow is Sunday
      +
Festival = Yes
            ↓
      Deployed AI Model
            ↓
Prediction:
"Expected cake sales: 120"

==========

🔗 How the Steps Connect

This is the most important part of a pipeline.

The output of one step becomes the input for another step.

STEP 1
Raw Dataset
      │
      ▼
STEP 2
Clean Data
      │
      ▼
STEP 3
Prepared Features
      │
      ▼
STEP 4
Trained Model
      │
      ▼
STEP 5
Evaluation Result
      │
      ├── Good ✅ → Deploy
      │
      └── Poor ❌ → Retrain / Improve

=========

🔁 Example of Automation

Without a pipeline, you might manually do this:

1. Upload data
2. Run cleaning code
3. Run training code
4. Check accuracy
5. Save model
6. Deploy model

Every time new data arrives, you may need to repeat the process.

With a pipeline

You define the workflow:

New Dataset
      ↓
Automatically Clean
      ↓
Automatically Train
      ↓
Automatically Evaluate
      ↓
If conditions are met
      ↓
Deploy Model

This makes the workflow repeatable and organized.

=========

🏗️ Pipeline Components

In Vertex AI Pipelines, you can think of each major step as a component.

For example:

Pipeline
│
├── Component 1: Data Preparation
│
├── Component 2: Model Training
│
├── Component 3: Model Evaluation
│
└── Component 4: Model Deployment

=========

Each component performs one specific task.

Component A
Output: Clean Data
       ↓
Component B
Input: Clean Data
Output: Trained Model
       ↓
Component C
Input: Trained Model
Output: Evaluation Metrics

Together:

Components + Connections + Execution Order = Pipeline

==========

🔀 Pipelines Can Also Make Decisions

A pipeline does not always have to be a simple straight line.

Example:

                 Train Model
                      ↓
                 Evaluate
                      ↓
              Accuracy Check
                 /        \
                /          \
             ≥ 90%          < 90%
               ↓              ↓
           Deploy          Retrain

So a pipeline can support workflow logic, depending on how it is designed.

==========

